course: politics
lesson: 36
title: Checking practice: true, false and not sure yet
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Every claim goes in one of three piles: TRUE (checked and shown real), FALSE (checked and shown wrong) and NOT SURE YET (nobody has checked), and the third pile is allowed.
explain: Think of a lost-property box: some things clearly belong to you, some clearly do not, and some you cannot tell until you look for your name inside; you would not grab a coat just because it looks like yours.
explain: Practice makes it quick: an amazing shark photo during a flood, "the election's cancelled" from one clip, a famous footballer's quote with no when or where, a chain message that orders you to forward it.
explain: When you were fooled, say "I got it wrong" out loud; when someone else was fooled, correct the fake kindly and in private, not the person.
explain: Many people share fakes to warn the people they love, not to lie, so laughing at them only makes them stop listening.
fact: A shark-on-a-flooded-motorway photo turns up after almost every big flood; it has been checked many times and is an old picture edited to add the shark.
fact: A big thing like a cancelled national election would be on every news channel and on the official elections website at once.
fact: Some false claims about voting days are spread on purpose to stop people voting, which is called voter suppression.
fact: Anyone can type words next to a famous face; a real quote usually comes with when and where it was said, and searching the exact words can find it.
fact: "Not sure yet" is an honest answer, and a small idea, like a council discussing pets, can grow into a bigger story as it is passed on.
fact: Death hoaxes about famous people spread often; saying "I was wrong, it was a fake" is what careful people do.
fact: Misinformation is false information shared by someone who thinks it is true; disinformation is made on purpose to trick.
fact: A kind private message such as "I think this one is old, here's where I checked" works better than laughing at someone in the group.
fact: Headlines often make a small study sound huge; the study may have tested few people or found something tiny, and usually says "more research is needed".
fact: Videos can be slowed down, cut or changed to make someone look bad, and it was reported that slowed-down clips of real politicians were shared this way.
fact: Forwarding a message cannot protect or hack a phone; chain messages spread by making people scared.
fact: Words in this lesson: "nesh" in the North and Midlands of England means always feeling the cold; "mardy" means grumpy or sulky; "now now" in South Africa means soon; "chur" in New Zealand means thanks or cool.
exercise: During a storm a cousin sends a photo of a shark swimming down a flooded motorway. What is the truth about this photo? | It is an old picture edited to add the shark | It is a real shark, which turns up in every big flood | Sharks often swim up the motorways in big storms | It was taken in this storm by a TV news crew on a boat
exercise: How can you check the shark photo quickly? | Search "shark flooded motorway fact check" | Ask the cousin if she is sure | Count how many people shared it | Zoom into the shark's teeth
exercise: A boy says the election is cancelled because his brother saw it on TikTok. Where should Mia check? | The official elections website or the main news | The TikTok account of the boy's brother, who saw it first | The comments under the clip, where people explain it | A friend in her class who has not heard about it yet
exercise: What is voter suppression? | Trying to stop people voting, for example with a wrong date | Counting votes very carefully | Voting in secret so nobody sees | Asking everyone in a class to vote
exercise: A footballer's photo says "School is a waste of time, just play." How can Priya check the quote? | Search the exact words and look for a real interview | Count how many kids shared it | Trust it, because his photo is real and footballers say things like that all the time | Ask the footballer's fans
exercise: Grandad read that dogs will be banned in flats "somewhere on the internet". What is the honest answer for now? | Not sure yet, until we check an official site | Definitely true, Grandad read it | Definitely false, the internet always lies, so nothing on it can be true about dogs | True, because it sounds like a real plan
exercise: Chloe told the class a singer had died, but he posted a video that morning. What is the best thing for her to do? | Say out loud "I was wrong, it was a fake" | Pretend she never said it | Say the video of the singer is fake | Stay home tomorrow so nobody asks
exercise: Auntie shared a fake flood video and her nephew laughed at her in the group. What would have worked better? | A kind private message showing where he checked | An even bigger laugh in the group chat, so that Auntie learns not to do it again | Saying nothing and letting it spread | Sharing it again to more people
exercise: Auntie thought the video was true when she shared it. Which word fits? | Misinformation | Disinformation on purpose | Satire or parody | Wartime propaganda
exercise: "Scientists say chocolate makes you smarter!" What should Ethan look for under the headline? | Who did the study, how many people, and what they found | The colour of the chocolate in the picture, since darker chocolate means a better study | How many likes the headline got | Whether the headline uses capital letters
exercise: A politician's voice sounds slow and slurred in a "Drunk at work!" clip. What is a good check? | Find the original full video and compare | Believe it because it looks real | Slow it down even more | Share it to see what others think
exercise: Which pile does "Our school will get a new pool next year" go in, if nobody knows who decided it? | Not sure | True | False | Joke
exercise: "Forward this to 10 people or your phone will be hacked tonight!" What is true? | Forwarding a message cannot protect or hack a phone | Your phone will be hacked if you don't forward it before midnight tonight | Forwarding to 10 people makes your phone safe | Only phones in group chats can be hacked
exercise: What is the best thing to do with that chain message? | Do not forward it, and tell friends kindly it is a chain message | Forward it to 10 people just in case | Forward it to 20 people to be extra safe, and ask each of them to send it to 20 more friends | Turn your phone off forever
test: In Sheffield: "Don't be nesh, it's only a storm — but look, a shark on the motorway!" What would a careful person do first? | Check it, since that shark photo is a known old fake | Share it to warn the whole city | Go out to see the shark | Call the zoo to report a missing shark, in case one has escaped in the flood water
test: What would happen if a national election really were cancelled? | Every news channel and the official elections site would say so | Only one TikTok clip would mention it, because the government would want to keep it quiet | Nobody would be told until voting day | It would only be in the comments
test: "she's well mardy cos I said that footballer quote's fake" — no interview has the words. Who is more likely right? | You, because a real quote can be found with when and where | She is, because his face is on it, and nobody would dare to use a footballer's face for a fake | Both, because quotes can be half real | Nobody, quotes can never be checked
test: "my grandad says dogs are banned in flats next yr, so it's true innit" — he cannot say where he read it. Which pile? | Not sure yet | True | False | A joke
test: Chloe says, "If I admit I was wrong, everyone will think I'm stupid." What is the better view? | Correcting yourself makes people trust you more | She is right, never admit any mistakes to anyone | Only teachers are allowed to say they were wrong | Saying sorry makes the fake true, so it is better to say nothing
test: "lol Auntie fell for a fake AGAIN" — what is the problem with this reply? | It shames her, so she may stop listening and leave | It is too kind to Auntie | It corrects the fake too gently | Nothing, laughing is the best correction, because people remember being laughed at for a long time
test: In Johannesburg: "I'll check it now now, but first let me forward it to everyone." What is the better order? | Check first, then decide whether to share | Forward first, then check later | Forward it and never check | Check only if someone complains
test: In a cartoon, a parrot repeats "The king has banned birthdays!" and the whole town panics, but the king never said it. What should the townspeople have asked? | Who first said it and where it came from | How loudly the parrot said it | What colour the parrot was | Whether the parrot liked birthdays
test: "chur bro, chocolate makes you smarter, says scientists, choccy for brekkie!" What is likely in the study itself? | A small result that says "might" and "more research is needed" | Proof that chocolate makes everyone a genius, whatever their age and however much they eat | A law that children must eat chocolate | Nothing, studies never mention chocolate
test: A clip of a politician sounds slurred. The original full video sounds normal. What happened? | The clip was slowed down or changed | The politician changed his voice later | The full video was faked to hide it all | Both videos are exactly the same as each other
test: The teacher says, "Ireland has a president." Which card? | TRUE | FALSE | NOT SURE | JOKE
test: "Forward this or ur phone gets hacked, 100% real, my cousin works in tech" — what is the best reply? | Forwarding cannot hack or protect a phone, so don't forward | Forward it quick, your cousin knows tech and would never send a fake message around | Forward it to twice as many people | Throw your phone away tonight
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the appropriate classification of a claim for which no verification has yet been undertaken? | It is to be regarded as not yet determined | It is to be regarded as established beyond doubt | It is to be regarded as false until it is proven | It is to be regarded as satire by its author
exam: It is asserted by a single video that a national election has been cancelled. Identify the source most appropriate for verification. | The official electoral website or the principal news outlets | The relative of the person who viewed the video, who is acquainted with its origin and its author | The comments appended beneath the video | Any person who has not yet heard of the matter
exam: Whereas a relative has circulated a fabricated video in the belief that it was genuine, select the response most consistent with good practice. | A courteous private communication indicating the means of verification | Public ridicule within the group in which it was circulated, so as to discourage any repetition | Further circulation of the video to additional persons | The removal of the relative from all communication
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning messages demanding onward transmission upon pain of a device being compromised. | The transmission or otherwise of such a message has no bearing upon the security of a device | Transmission to ten persons protects the device | Failure to transmit invariably results in compromise of the device and of every device in the same group | Only devices belonging to groups can be compromised
